[{:db/id -1
 :adr/id "adr-2609091900-splitting-a-protocol-forks-the-contract"
 :adr/title "Splitting a protocol forks the contract"
 :adr/status "accepted"
 :adr/date "2026-09-09"
 :adr/related "adr-2609091200-a-definition-is-the-unit-of-a-library"
 :adr/body "Measured 2026-09-09, while splitting kotoba.lang.process under ADR-2609091200.

A definition can be moved to its own repo. A PROTOCOL cannot, not without
deciding who owns the contract, because a protocol's identity is what reify and
extend-type dispatch on. Two namespaces that each declare IFilesystem declare
TWO protocols, and an object implementing one does not satisfy the other.

## How it surfaced

kotoba.lang.process-host reifies kotoba.lang.process/IProcess. Running the
process family's own suite against the split gave

  No implementation of method: :spawn! of protocol:
  #'kotoba.process.iprocess/IProcess found for class:
  kotoba.lang.process_host$os_spawn$reify__2388

which is the split working exactly as written and being wrong anyway.

## What is already published in this state

Counted across orgs/, mentions of each protocol outside the namespace it was
split from:

  fs/IFilesystem        21 files -- kotoba-lang/store, amu, lint, and more
  http/IHttp            79 files
  process/IProcess       8 files -- process-host, kotoba/git_adapter, launcher
  io/IReader, IWriter    0 files outside the split's own repos

fs and http were published before this was measured. NOTHING EXISTING BREAKS:
kotoba.lang.fs and kotoba.lang.http keep their protocols and their implementers,
and the splits are additive. What is broken is ADOPTION -- code that starts
using kotoba.fs cannot hand a kotoba.lang.fs-host implementation to it, and
finds out at runtime rather than at compile time.

io is unaffected: its two protocols have no implementer outside the split.

## The three ways out, and why none is a small edit

1. The split's protocol repo re-exports the ORIGINAL protocol instead of
   declaring one. One contract, everything satisfies it -- and the split now
   depends on the namespace it was supposed to replace.

2. The definition repo OWNS the protocol and the old namespace re-exports it.
   Also one contract, and the right end state, but it edits namespaces with 21
   and 79 consumers.

3. Two protocols, and implementers migrate with the callers. Coherent, and it
   makes the facade something other than a drop-in.

This is an owner-level choice about migration order, not a mechanical one, so
no further protocol-bearing family is published until it is made. Families
without protocols -- string, coll, edn, spec -- are unaffected and complete.

## What this does not change

The eight families already split still match their unsplit suites exactly, and
that measurement stands: the split does not change an answer WITHIN a family.
This is about what happens at the boundary between the split and code that was
written against the original."
